# What you need to *really* know (summary)
- **Bubble sort:** repeatedly swap adjacent out-of-order elements. Simple, stable, in-place, O(n²) average/worst, O(n) best (with early-exit). Good for tiny arrays or mostly-sorted data; otherwise slow.
- **Selection sort:** repeatedly select min (or max) and swap into place. In-place, **not stable** (unless modified), always O(n²) comparisons, O(n) swaps. Good when writes are expensive (few swaps).
- **Insertion sort:** build sorted prefix by inserting next element into it (shift elements). Stable, in-place, O(n²) average/worst, O(n) best, very good for small arrays or nearly-sorted data; often used in hybrid sorts (e.g., introsort/timsort) for small chunks.

