# 1) Bubble Sort
## Algorithm (standard)
Repeat n-1 passes. On each pass, compare adjacent pairs and swap if out of order. After k passes, largest k elements are in final place at the right.

## Loop invariant
At the start of pass `i` (0-based), the last `i` elements are the largest `i` elements in sorted order.

## Complexity & properties
- Comparisons: ~n(n-1)/2 (always) → Θ(n²)
- Swaps: up to Θ(n²) worst; O(n) best for mostly-sorted? depends on variant
- Space: O(1)
- Stable: yes
- In-place: yes
- Adaptive: can be with early-exit

## Optimized bubble (early-exit + shrinking)
Stop when no swaps happen on a pass; also reduce the high bound each pass.

## Code: basic and optimized
```c
// bubble_basic.c
#include <stdio.h>

void bubble_basic(int a[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; ++j) {
            if (a[j] > a[j+1]) {
                int t = a[j]; a[j] = a[j+1]; a[j+1] = t;
            }
        }
    }
}

void bubble_optimized(int a[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; ++i) {
        int swapped = 0;
        for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; ++j) {
            if (a[j] > a[j+1]) {
                int t = a[j]; a[j] = a[j+1]; a[j+1] = t;
                swapped = 1;
            }
        }
        if (!swapped) break; // array already sorted
    }
}

void print_arr(int a[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) printf("%d ", a[i]);
    printf("\n");
}

int main(void) {
    int arr[] = {5,2,9,1,5,6};
    int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);

    bubble_optimized(arr, n);
    print_arr(arr, n);
    return 0;
}
```

