# 2) Selection Sort
## Algorithm
For i from 0 to n-2:
- find index `min_idx` of minimum element in `a[i..n-1]`
- swap a[i] and a[min_idx]

## Loop invariant
At start of iteration `i`, subarray `a[0..i-1]` contains the i smallest elements in sorted order.

## Complexity & properties
- Comparisons: Θ(n²) always (exactly n(n-1)/2)
- Swaps: exactly n-1 swaps (or fewer if min already at i) — good if writes are expensive
- Space: O(1)
- Stable: **not** stable (swap can change order of equal elements)
- In-place: yes

## Stable variant (shifting instead of swapping)
Find min element, then **shift** the elements between `i` and `min_idx-1` one position right and place min at `i` — this preserves order of equals. That makes swaps O(n) per pass (worse writes), but stable.

## Code: standard and stable selection
```c
// selection.c
#include <stdio.h>

void selection_sort(int a[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n-1; ++i) {
        int min_idx = i;
        for (int j = i+1; j < n; ++j)
            if (a[j] < a[min_idx]) min_idx = j;
        // swap a[i] and a[min_idx]
        int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[min_idx]; a[min_idx] = t;
    }
}

// Stable selection using shifting
void selection_sort_stable(int a[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; ++i) {
        int min_idx = i;
        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; ++j)
            if (a[j] < a[min_idx]) min_idx = j;
        int key = a[min_idx];
        // shift right the block a[i..min_idx-1]
        while (min_idx > i) {
            a[min_idx] = a[min_idx - 1];
            --min_idx;
        }
        a[i] = key;
    }
}

void print_arr(int a[], int n) { for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) printf("%d ", a[i]); printf("\n"); }

int main(void) {
    int arr[] = {4,5,3,2,4,1};
    int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);

    selection_sort(arr, n);
    print_arr(arr, n);

    int arr2[] = {4,5,3,2,4,1};
    selection_sort_stable(arr2, n);
    print_arr(arr2, n);

    return 0;
}
```

