# 3) Insertion Sort
## Algorithm
For `i = 1..n-1`: pick `key = a[i]`, insert it into sorted subarray `a[0..i-1]` by shifting larger elements right, place `key`.

## Loop invariant
At start of each iteration `i`, `a[0..i-1]` is sorted.

## Complexity & properties
- Best: O(n) when array already sorted (only comparisons)
- Average/Worst: O(n²)
- Space: O(1)
- Stable: yes
- In-place: yes
- Very efficient for small n (n up to few hundreds depending on context) and nearly-sorted arrays.

## Binary insertion sort (reduce comparisons)
Use binary search to find insertion point (still shifts elements though) — reduces comparisons to O(n log n) but shifts keep O(n²) moves in worst case. Usually not worth unless comparisons expensive.

## Code: standard, with step-print for learning, and binary insertion
```c
// insertion.c
#include <stdio.h>

// standard insertion sort
void insertion_sort(int a[], int n) {
    for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
        int key = a[i];
        int j = i - 1;
        while (j >= 0 && a[j] > key) {
            a[j+1] = a[j]; // shift right
            --j;
        }
        a[j+1] = key;
    }
}

// insertion sort with binary search to find insertion index
int binary_search_insert(int a[], int item, int low, int high) {
    // find index to insert item into a[low..high] (assumes sorted)
    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
        if (a[mid] == item) return mid + 1; // insert after equal to keep stable
        else if (a[mid] < item) low = mid + 1;
        else high = mid - 1;
    }
    return low;
}

void insertion_sort_binary(int a[], int n) {
    for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
        int key = a[i];
        int loc = binary_search_insert(a, key, 0, i - 1);
        // shift elements to make room
        for (int j = i - 1; j >= loc; --j)
            a[j+1] = a[j];
        a[loc] = key;
    }
}

void print_arr(int a[], int n) { for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) printf("%d ", a[i]); printf("\n"); }

int main(void) {
    int arr[] = {9,5,1,4,3};
    int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);
    insertion_sort(arr, n);
    print_arr(arr, n);

    int arr2[] = {9,5,1,4,3};
    insertion_sort_binary(arr2, n);
    print_arr(arr2, n);

    return 0;
}
```

